{{-- Pre-paint auth bounce for the app pages.

    This is the one place in the app that is allowed to touch document.cookie
    directly, and it stays a plain script rather than a client block on purpose:
    it must run before the runtime exists and before anything paints, so no
    composable is available to it and hydrating would be far too late — a
    signed-out visitor would see the app shell first.

    Keeping it in ONE partial rather than copied into five pages is what makes
    the strict-mode report legible: the DOM guard names this file, and any
    second file appearing in that report is a real regression rather than one of
    five known copies.

    It used to read the token from localStorage and, if the cookie was missing,
    write the cookie and reload the whole page so the server could see it. All
    of that is gone. The cookie IS the session now — the session store writes it
    at sign-in and every server-rendered page authenticates from it — so there
    is nothing to mirror and nothing to reload for. That removes a full document
    load from first sign-in.

    `#auth-pending` is rendered by the server when the cookie did not
    authenticate, which means the token is stale rather than absent. Reading it
    here, from the DOM, is deliberate: the server cannot distinguish "no cookie
    yet" from "bad cookie" at render time, and moving this into the server's own
    signed-out branch would bounce a first-visit user in a loop. --}}
<script>
(function () {
  if (!/(^|;)\s*loghq_token=/.test(document.cookie)) {
    window.location.replace('/login')
    return
  }
  if (document.getElementById('auth-pending')) {
    document.cookie = 'loghq_token=; path=/; max-age=0'
    window.location.replace('/login')
  }
})()
</script>
